<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<openrocket version="1.11" creator="OpenRocket 26.xx-SNAPSHOT-5f164fd0e">
  <rocket>
    <name>Simulation extensions</name>
    <id>af9a088d-52eb-4bfc-a982-7791836411a9</id>
    <axialoffset method="absolute">0.0</axialoffset>
    <position type="absolute">0.0</position>
    <comment>This design demonstrates active roll control and air-start using simulation extensions.  The main fins are slightly misaligned, which causes roll to occur.

The fin set named CONTROL acts as a canard to control the rocket&#39;s roll.  The simulation listener checks the current roll rate during flight and applies a PID controller to control the angle of these fins, counter-acting the roll caused by the main fins.

This is the first configuration of the Iso-Haisu hybrid rocket which was used to demonstrate active control of a rocket by the Finnish Astronautical Society.  This canard-style control failed to work due to interference between the control and main fins, which is not simulated by OpenRocket.
-----------------------------------------------------------
Simulation #1 has no extensions added.
Simulation #2 has an active roll control extension.
Simulation #3 has both active roll control and air-start extensions.
The code for both extensions can be found in core/src/net/sf/openrocket/simulation/extension/example/
Their configuration GUIs are in swing/src/net/sf/openrocket/simulation/extension/example/</comment>
    <designer>Sampo Niskanen (based on HyperLOC 1600)</designer>
    <designtype>original</designtype>
    <revision>Switched from plain listeners to listeners encapsulated in extensions. by Joe Pfeiffer, January 2023</revision>
